1
00:00:00,401 --> 00:00:05,951
This is the Rosso Scenario Desk, an independent concept
I built for the tem Senior Product Manager role.

2
00:00:06,052 --> 00:00:10,752
Everything is synthetic.
Fictional assets, fictional prices, and no trades.

3
00:00:10,852 --> 00:00:15,416
I load one settlement day of half-hourly
demand, generation and forecast data.

4
00:00:15,515 --> 00:00:19,957
Checks come first. Coverage asks whether
every reading is actually there.

5
00:00:20,056 --> 00:00:23,974
Forecast error is reported separately,
and split by model version.

6
00:00:24,075 --> 00:00:29,612
The clean day concludes. An unforecast demand spike
adds about nine hundred and seventy pounds.

7
00:00:29,712 --> 00:00:34,382
An outage costs more, because the forecast
is only corrected after a detection lag.

8
00:00:34,483 --> 00:00:39,457
Every bar traces back to the raw CSV lines behind it,
and to the scenario change applied.

9
00:00:39,558 --> 00:00:44,655
Assumptions are editable. Cutting detection lag
to one period cuts the outage cost sharply.

10
00:00:44,756 --> 00:00:46,875
Now a day with a missing meter batch.

11
00:00:46,977 --> 00:00:51,838
Missing readings are not zero. So every total
is withheld, and the gaps are hatched.

12
00:00:51,938 --> 00:00:54,815
The trace shows the blank reading
that caused the block.

13
00:00:54,915 --> 00:01:00,569
Stale or conflicting inputs go to a person. Each decision
is recorded, then the results conclude.

14
00:01:00,669 --> 00:01:06,286
Finally, I export a decision memo and a scenario CSV,
with every source row ID.

15
00:01:06,386 --> 00:01:09,986
The decision itself stays with an accountable person,
not the tool.

